  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1\n\nThe warm front remains draped along the I-90 corridor as of 1:30 PM \nthis afternoon. It should nudge a bit more north and east, clearing \nthe CWA heading into the late afternoon hours. Temperatures south of \nthe warm front have surge into the mid-70s to lower 80s as expected, \nalong with dew points getting into the 60s. This will set the stage \nfor increasing instability, as MLCAPE likely reaches 500-800 J/kg by \nlate afternoon. Bulk, deep layer shear is increasing and should be \n40-45 kts over north-central NY heading into the early evening \nhours. A weak perturbation is observed crossing Lake America and Erie \nat this time. This feature will reach our forecast area between 4-8 \nPM, and initiate thunderstorms in the rather favorable environment. \nThis event is missing strong forcing to enhance the convective \nactivity. With that said, there remains a marginal risk for an \nisolated strong to severe storm to bring some hail or damaging wind \ngusts. Considering the robust low level shear and helicity present, \nalso cannot completely rule out a brief spin up (tornado).\n\nThe thunderstorm activity looks to weaken and fade to just lingering \nshowers after about 9 PM based on the latest CAMs.\n\nThe rest of tonight will see increasing clouds from north to south \nand very warm overnight lows in the upper 50s to low 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nA northwest flow pattern arrives over the area on Wednesday, with \nplenty of moisture trapped in the low levels of the atmosphere. \nThere could be a few sprinkles or light showers around at times. \nBased on current upstream satellite observations over northern \nlower Michigan, it would not be surprising to see a stratus or \nstrato-cumulus cloud deck form over Central NY and extend into the \nnorthern Tier of NE PA through most of the day on Wednesday. There \ncould be a few breaks of sun at times, especially further south and \neast across NE PA. High temperatures will depend on just how thick \nand persistent the cloud cover is. The latest guidance is suggesting \nhighs in the 60s for Central NY, with upper 60s to mid-70s in NE PA.\n\nSurface high pressure builds in from the north for Thursday. After \nsome lingering morning clouds it should turn mostly sunny over the \nregion. It will be seasonably mild with highs in the 60s for most.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3\n\nThe next weather system will quickly move into the area Thursday \nnight into Friday, as surface low tracks just north of the area, \nnear Lakes Erie and America. This will bring a period of steady, \nwarm air advection rainfall along the warm front Thursday night. \nThere could be a few brief dry slots on Friday, but there will also \nbe plenty of showers around in the robust southwesterly flow. The \ncold front then translates east across the area during the day on \nFriday with additional periods of rain into the afternoon or evening \nhours. The winds turn northwesterly behind the front and increase \nbetween 15-25 mph...gusts could reach 30-40 mph Friday night into \nSaturday with the deep mixing and steep low level lapse rates up to \nnearly 750mb level. Wind advisories may eventually be issued for \nportions of the area if confidence in higher gusts increases.\n\nAs cold air advection strengthens, 850mb temperatures drop to -8C \nto -10C late Friday night and early Saturday. This could allow \nfor wet snow showers to mix in, or become the predominant \nprecipitation type. It is too early to determine how much \nmoisture will be left on the back side of the system and just \nhow much the lakes will get involved. With that said, we cannot \nrule out some minor, localized slushy accumulations over the \nhigher elevations. Saturday will be downright blustery and cold \nfor mid October standards, with wind chills in the upper 10s to \n20s in the morning hours. High temperatures only reach the upper\n30s to mid-40s. Now is the time to prepare for this cold blast,\nmake sure your heating source is ready to go for the season and\nhave proper clothing on hand if you need to be outside Friday \nnight into Saturday. It will be another cold one Saturday night,\nbut winds diminish under 10 mph as high pressure builds. \nOvernight lows dip down into the mid-20s to lower 30s areawide.\n\nLooking ahead, a large ridge of high pressure looks to keep our \nweather dry with a gradual warming trend Sunday through the middle \nof next week. There should be plenty of sunshine most days.",
